# Booting and Setting Up a Raspberry Pi with Linux

## 1. Prepare the MicroSD Card

- Download [Raspberry Pi Imager](https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/) for your OS.
- Insert microSD card (8GB+ recommended).
- Open Raspberry Pi Imager.
    - Choose OS: "Raspberry Pi OS (32-bit)" or another preferred Linux distro.
    - Choose Storage: Your microSD card.
    - Click "Write".
- Eject and insert the microSD card into your Raspberry Pi.

## 2. First Boot

- Connect monitor, keyboard, mouse.
- Plug in power; Pi will boot automatically.
- Follow on-screen setup instructions (language, Wi-Fi, update).

## 3. (Optional) Enable SSH or Headless Setup

- To enable SSH before first boot, create an empty file named `ssh` (no extension) in the boot partition of the SD card.
- For headless Wi-Fi, add a file named `wpa_supplicant.conf` with your Wi-Fi details.

## 4. Update System

Open a terminal and run:
```bash
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
```

## 5. Ready for React or Any Linux Software!

You're now running Linux on your Raspberry Pi and can install other packages (like Node.js, npm, etc.) to run React or other apps.
